# Revision History # {#revision-history}

## R3 (2026-10-12) ## {#revision-r3}

- Update the proposal for the C++26 draft N5050.
- Compare current and proposed interfaces for calls, references, ownership, and pack operations.
- Account for type and value pack indexing without changing which expansion handles an existing pack occurrence.
- Distinguish a new homogeneous parameter pack from an ordinary expansion of an enclosing pack.
- Narrow the new lambda genericness rule to homogeneous declarations.
- Separate the preferred comma-less varargs reclamation from the compatibility decision it requires.
- Defer explicit lengths.

## R2 (2019-10-05) ## {#revision-r2}

- Rebased wording against N4830.
- Added discussion on the impact of making varargs commas mandatory.

## R1 (2019-03-11) ## {#revision-r1}

- Rebased wording against N4800.
- Added wording for signature changes.
- Removed wording permitting empty template parameter lists for template declarations.
- Added feature test macro.
- Added discussion of alternative designs aimed at addressing or avoiding confusion with explicit specializations.
- Added straw polls from EWGI in Kona.
- Added acknowledgments.
- Minor corrections to examples.

## R0 (2018-10-08) ## {#revision-r0}

- Initial draft. [[P1219R2]]

# Introduction # {#introduction}

This paper proposes function parameter packs whose parameters share one declared element type and use ordinary parameter initialization:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
template <class T> // T is not a parameter pack.
void foo(T... vs); // vs is a parameter pack.
</xmp>

C++ already permits a homogeneous template parameter pack:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
// Legal since C++11: T is not a parameter pack, but vs is.
template <class T, T... vs>
void foo();
</xmp>

The first pack would contain ordinary function parameters initialized at the call, with a length deduced at compile time and values that may be computed at runtime. The second contains constant template arguments. [[CXX-VARIADIC]]

This revision proposes a single homogeneous pack at the end of a function's parameter list. Its declaration makes the function an abbreviated function template, with no explicit template head needed when there are no template parameters to declare. Each specialization has an ordinary function type with a fixed number of parameters and is distinguished in part by the pack length. Existing packs, pack indexing, and nested expansions retain their meanings, including in lambdas.

The named spelling <code>int... values</code> is unambiguous. Supporting unnamed packs as well requires the separate compatibility decision in [[#compatibility]], because <code>int...</code> retains its deprecated C-varargs meaning in C++26. [[CXX-VARARGS-DEPRECATION]]

# Examples # {#examples}

The current and proposed definitions are independent alternatives.

## Pass existing points and braced initializers ## {#example-points}

Consider a <code>Point</code> with integer coordinates:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
struct Point {
    int x;
    int y;
};
</xmp>

<code>append_points</code> adds points to a path. A constrained pack already accepts separate <code>Point</code> arguments. The proposed declaration gives each parameter a fixed type so callers can also pass braced initializers without repeating <code>Point</code>:

<table class="data example-comparison">
<caption>The same append operation with deduced or fixed Point parameter types.</caption>
<thead><tr><th scope=col>Current C++</th><th scope=col>With homogeneous packs</th></tr></thead>
<tbody><tr><td>
<xmp class=language-cpp>
void append_points(
    std::vector<Point>& path,
    std::same_as<Point> auto... points) {
    (path.push_back(points), ...);
}
</xmp>
</td><td>
<xmp class=language-cpp>
void append_points(
    std::vector<Point>& path,
    Point... points) {
    (path.push_back(points), ...);
}
</xmp>
</td></tr></tbody>
</table>

For a <code>std::vector&lt;Point&gt; path</code> and named <code>Point</code> objects <code>origin</code>, <code>corner</code>, and <code>finish</code>:

<table class=data>
<caption>Calls to the two append_points definitions.</caption>
<thead><tr><th scope=col>Call</th><th scope=col>Current C++</th><th scope=col>With homogeneous packs</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><code>append_points(path)</code></td><td>Appends no points</td><td>Appends no points</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>append_points(path, origin)</code></td><td>Appends one point</td><td>Appends one point</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>append_points(path, origin, corner, finish)</code></td><td>Appends three points</td><td>Appends three points</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>append_points(path, origin, {2, 3}, {4, 5})</code></td><td>Error: cannot deduce types for the braced arguments</td><td>Appends three points</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>append_points(path, origin, Point{2, 3}, Point{4, 5})</code></td><td>Appends three points</td><td>Appends three points</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

A <code>std::convertible_to&lt;Point&gt;</code> constraint cannot resolve this deduction failure, because it needs argument types before checking conversions. A fixed <code>Point</code> parameter supplies the destination type for ordinary parameter initialization of each pack element. [[CXX-DEDUCTION-CALL]]

An initializer-list interface also accepts these braced elements in current C++:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
void append_points(
    std::vector<Point>& path,
    std::initializer_list<Point> points) {
    path.insert(
        path.end(), points.begin(), points.end());
}

void build_path() {
    std::vector<Point> path;
    Point origin{0, 0};

    append_points(path, {origin, {2, 3}, {4, 5}});
    append_points(path, {});
}
</xmp>

That interface groups the points into one list argument. The proposed pack combines separate arguments with a fixed destination type for each. Both the list and proposed pack reject <code>{2.5, 3}</code> because it would narrow <code>Point::x</code>.

## Keep the count in the result type ## {#example-count}

<code>point_block</code> returns an array with one element per point, even when the coordinates are computed at runtime. A typed array reference already accepts braced elements and deduces their count in current C++. The proposed pack accepts the points as separate arguments:

<table class="data example-comparison">
<caption>Returning an array whose extent is the number of points.</caption>
<thead><tr><th scope=col>Current C++</th><th scope=col>With homogeneous packs</th></tr></thead>
<tbody><tr><td>
<xmp class=language-cpp>
template <std::size_t N>
auto point_block(const Point (&points)[N]) {
    return std::to_array(points);
}

void record_points(Point origin) {
    auto block =
        point_block({origin, {2, 3}, {4, 5}});
    static_assert(
        std::tuple_size_v<decltype(block)> == 3);
}
</xmp>
</td><td>
<xmp class=language-cpp>
auto point_block(Point... points) {
    return std::array<
        Point, sizeof...(points)>{points...};
}

void record_points(Point origin) {
    auto block =
        point_block(origin, {2, 3}, {4, 5});
    static_assert(
        std::tuple_size_v<decltype(block)> == 3);

    auto empty = point_block();
    static_assert(
        std::tuple_size_v<decltype(empty)> == 0);
}
</xmp>
</td></tr></tbody>
</table>

Both three-point calls return <code>std::array&lt;Point, 3&gt;</code>. The number of points determines the extent independently of their coordinate values. Both interfaces reject a braced <code>{2.5, 3}</code> element as narrowing. The typed array-reference declaration cannot deduce <code>N</code> from <code>point_block({})</code>, though a zero-argument overload can supply the empty case. Built-in arrays allow neither zero bounds nor reference elements. An rvalue array-reference interface can transfer move-only elements. [[CXX-DEDUCTION-CALL]] [[CXX-ARRAYS]] [[CXX-LIST-INIT]]

A constrained pack also provides <code>sizeof...</code> but retains the braced-argument deduction limit shown above. A typed array reference provides braces and count through one grouped argument. The homogeneous pack combines separate fixed-type parameters, braced initialization, and a count in one declaration that also accepts an empty call. Direct <code>std::array</code> construction or <code>std::to_array</code> suffices when the caller only needs an array.

## Modify the original points ## {#example-references}

The proposed <code>translate</code> takes <code>Point&amp;</code> parameters to modify the caller's points. A constrained reference pack already provides the same behavior for the named <code>Point</code> lvalues shown here:

<table class="data example-comparison">
<caption>Translating the caller's Point objects through reference parameters.</caption>
<thead><tr><th scope=col>Current C++</th><th scope=col>With homogeneous packs</th></tr></thead>
<tbody><tr><td>
<xmp class=language-cpp>
void translate(
    [[maybe_unused]] int dx,
    [[maybe_unused]] int dy,
    std::same_as<Point> auto&... points) {
    ((points.x += dx, points.y += dy), ...);
}
</xmp>
</td><td>
<xmp class=language-cpp>
void translate(
    [[maybe_unused]] int dx,
    [[maybe_unused]] int dy, Point&... points) {
    ((points.x += dx, points.y += dy), ...);
}
</xmp>
</td></tr></tbody>
</table>

The same call modifies the original objects with either definition:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
void move_endpoints() {
    Point start{0, 0};
    Point finish{4, 5};

    translate(2, 3, start, finish);
    assert(start.x == 2 && start.y == 3);
    assert(finish.x == 6 && finish.y == 8);
}
</xmp>

The proposed form states ordinary <code>Point&amp;</code> binding directly. Neither definition accepts <code>translate(2, 3, {0, 0})</code> or a <code>const Point</code> argument. An initializer list of reference wrappers is another current-language option, discussed in [[#initializer-lists]].

## Transfer ownership ## {#example-ownership}

<code>translate</code> borrows points. A queue instead takes ownership of its jobs:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
struct Job {
    int id;
};
</xmp>

<code>enqueue</code> receives its <code>std::unique_ptr&lt;Job&gt;</code> arguments by value. The current definition constrains their deduced types, whereas the homogeneous declaration states the type directly:

<table class="data example-comparison">
<caption>Taking ownership of jobs before moving them into a queue.</caption>
<thead><tr><th scope=col>Current C++</th><th scope=col>With homogeneous packs</th></tr></thead>
<tbody><tr><td>
<xmp class=language-cpp>
void enqueue(
    std::vector<std::unique_ptr<Job>>& queue,
    std::same_as<
        std::unique_ptr<Job>> auto... jobs) {
    (queue.push_back(std::move(jobs)), ...);
}
</xmp>
</td><td>
<xmp class=language-cpp>
void enqueue(
    std::vector<std::unique_ptr<Job>>& queue,
    std::unique_ptr<Job>... jobs) {
    (queue.push_back(std::move(jobs)), ...);
}
</xmp>
</td></tr></tbody>
</table>

<code>submit_jobs</code> transfers an existing job and one created at the call. The same call works with either definition:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
void submit_jobs() {
    std::vector<std::unique_ptr<Job>> queue;
    auto first = std::make_unique<Job>(Job{1});

    enqueue(
        queue,
        std::move(first),
        std::make_unique<Job>(Job{2}));

    assert(!first);
    assert(queue.size() == 2);
    assert(queue[0]->id == 1 && queue[1]->id == 2);
}
</xmp>

Both definitions transfer ownership through by-value parameters. The proposed pack names their type directly and uses ordinary parameter initialization. The initializer-list alternative in [[#initializer-lists]] moves through mutable wrapper members.

## Choose a fixed or deduced element type ## {#example-deduction}

Fixing the destination type also chooses which conversions the interface accepts. <code>sum_fixed</code> fixes <code>int</code>, while <code>total</code> deduces one <code>T</code> from all applicable arguments unless the caller supplies it explicitly:

<table class="data example-comparison">
<caption>Fixed <code>int</code> in <code>sum_fixed</code> and a type parameter <code>T</code> in <code>total</code>.</caption>
<thead><tr><th scope=col>Current C++</th><th scope=col>With homogeneous packs</th></tr></thead>
<tbody><tr><td>
<xmp class=language-cpp>
int sum_fixed(std::initializer_list<int> values) {
    return std::accumulate(
        values.begin(), values.end(), 0);
}

template <class T>
T total(std::initializer_list<T> values) {
    return std::accumulate(
        values.begin(), values.end(), T{});
}

void add_values() {
    sum_fixed({1, 2, 3});
    total({1, 2, 3});
    sum_fixed({});
    total<int>({});

    // Error: narrowing
    sum_fixed({1, 2.5});
    // Error: conflicting T
    total({1, 2.5});
    // Error: narrowing
    total<int>({1, 2.5});
}
</xmp>
</td><td>
<xmp class=language-cpp>
int sum_fixed(int... values) {
    return (0 + ... + values);
}

template <class T>
T total(T... values) {
    return (T{} + ... + values);
}

void add_values() {
    sum_fixed(1, 2, 3);
    total(1, 2, 3);
    sum_fixed();
    total<int>();

    // Converts 2.5 to int
    sum_fixed(1, 2.5);
    // Error: conflicting T
    total(1, 2.5);
    // Converts 2.5 to int
    total<int>(1, 2.5);
}
</xmp>
</td></tr></tbody>
</table>

The initializer-list versions of <code>sum_fixed</code> and <code>total&lt;int&gt;</code> reject <code>2.5</code> as narrowing, while the proposed function parameters permit its conversion to <code>int</code>. Neither <code>total({})</code> nor the proposed <code>total()</code> can deduce <code>T</code>.

A <code>std::convertible_to</code> pack can also accept mixed source types while retaining the count. The constraint checks conversion availability and imposes semantic requirements without performing the conversions. The following implementation forwards each argument to an ordinary <code>int</code> parameter for conversion inside the body: [[CXX-CONVERTIBLE]]

<xmp class=language-cpp>
template <class... Args>
    requires (std::convertible_to<Args, int> && ...)
int sum_converted(Args&&... values) {
    return (0 + ... + [](int value) {
        return value;
    }(std::forward<Args>(values)));
}
</xmp>

Both <code>sum_converted(1, 2.5)</code> and the proposed <code>sum_fixed(1, 2.5)</code> return 3. Accepting that conversion is a policy choice: the initializer-list interface rejects the loss of the fractional part.

In <code>sum_converted</code>, different deduced <code>Args</code> produce different specializations and conversion occurs inside the body. The proposed <code>sum_fixed</code> initializes its <code>int</code> parameters before entering the body. Calls with the same count select the same specialization regardless of their source argument types. The braced-initializer distinction appears in [[#example-points]]. [[CXX-DEDUCTION-CALL]]

## Pack indexing ## {#example-indexing}

Homogeneous packs also use existing pack operations, such as C++26 pack indexing. A count constraint works in both forms:

<table class="data example-comparison">
<caption>Selecting a sample with a compile-time bounds constraint.</caption>
<thead><tr><th scope=col>Current C++</th><th scope=col>With homogeneous packs</th></tr></thead>
<tbody><tr><td>
<xmp class=language-cpp>
template <std::size_t I>
double select_sample(
    std::same_as<double> auto... samples)
    requires (I < sizeof...(samples)) {
    return samples...[I];
}
</xmp>
</td><td>
<xmp class=language-cpp>
template <std::size_t I>
double select_sample(double... samples)
    requires (I < sizeof...(samples)) {
    return samples...[I];
}
</xmp>
</td></tr></tbody>
</table>

The caller supplies the index and deduction supplies the pack length. For a runtime <code>double reading</code>, <code>select_sample&lt;1&gt;(reading, 0.5, 1.0)</code> selects the second sample in either version. Index 3 fails the constraint.

## Lambdas and function addresses ## {#example-callbacks}

For a lambda that multiplies a varying number of <code>double</code> factors, the current definition constrains its deduced parameter types. The proposed form declares them directly:

<table class="data example-comparison">
<caption>A generic lambda with one numeric parameter type.</caption>
<thead><tr><th scope=col>Current C++</th><th scope=col>With homogeneous packs</th></tr></thead>
<tbody><tr><td>
<xmp class=language-cpp>
auto product = [](
    std::same_as<double> auto... factors) {
    return (1.0 * ... * factors);
};
</xmp>
</td><td>
<xmp class=language-cpp>
auto product = [](double... factors) {
    return (1.0 * ... * factors);
};
</xmp>
</td></tr></tbody>
</table>

Both generic lambdas accept <code>product(0.5, 0.8, 0.9)</code>. The proposed form also converts integer arguments to <code>double</code>.

To use the proposed <code>sum_fixed</code> as a callback that adds two integers, supply a pointer type with two <code>int</code> parameters. That target selects the corresponding specialization, as with existing variadic templates:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
void use_callback() {
    int (*add_pair)(int, int) = sum_fixed;
    int combined = add_pair(2, 3);

    auto address = &sum_fixed; // Error: undetermined count
}
</xmp>

## Existing packs keep their meanings ## {#example-existing-packs}

<code>print_line</code> still accepts text, integers, and other types through <code>auto...</code>. In <code>make_receiver</code>, the returned lambda expands the enclosing <code>Ts</code> into a fixed parameter list:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
void print_line(auto... values) {
    (std::cout << ... << values) << '\n';
}

template <class... Ts>
auto make_receiver() {
    return [](Ts... values) {
        return sizeof...(values);
    };
}

void use_existing_packs() {
    print_line("count=", 3, ", ready=", true);

    auto receive = make_receiver<int, double>();
    receive(1, 2.5);
    receive(1); // Error: missing the double argument
}
</xmp>

## Scope of this revision ## {#example-scope}

This revision permits one trailing homogeneous pack, optionally preceded by ordinary parameters as in <code>translate</code> and <code>enqueue</code>. A C ellipsis cannot follow the pack. The following declarations do not meet those rules:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
void misplaced(int... values, int last);
void two_packs(int... left, double... right);
void trailing_varargs(int... values, ...);
</xmp>

<code>sum_fixed&lt;3&gt;(1, 2, 3)</code> cannot specify the implicit count as a template argument.

A homogeneous pack declares parameters of the function itself or of a lambda's call operator. The declarations <code>using Callback = void(int... values);</code> and <code>void register_callback(void (*callback)(int... values));</code> remain ill-formed. Ordinary pack expansions in aliases and nested function types are unchanged.

# Motivation and Scope # {#motivation}

The examples combine ordinary initialization of separate parameters with a count available during template instantiation. Constrained packs, initializer lists, and typed array references offer different combinations of these properties.

## Meeting user expectations ## {#symmetry}
<p>C++26 permits homogeneous template parameter packs but not homogeneous function parameter packs. [[CXX-VARIADIC]] [[CXX-AUTO]]</p>
<table class=data>
<caption>Parameter-pack declarations in C++26. Each declaration is independent, and the ill-formed function-parameter form is proposed.</caption>
    <tr><th></th><th>Template parameter pack</th><th>Function parameter pack</th></tr>
    <tr>
        <th>Heterogeneous</th>
        <td class=code-valid>
<xmp class=language-cpp>
// OK:
template <auto... vs>
void f();
</xmp>
        </td>
        <td class=code-valid>
<xmp class=language-cpp>
// OK:
void f(auto... vs);
</xmp>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <th>Homogeneous</th>
        <td class=code-valid>
<xmp class=language-cpp>
// OK:
template <class Type, Type... vs>
void f();
</xmp>
        </td>
        <td class=code-invalid>
<xmp class=language-cpp>
// Ill-formed:
template <class Type>
void f(Type... vs);
</xmp>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

## Greater expressivity ## {#expressivity}

<p>R2 cited eight <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/">Stack Overflow</a> questions asking how to write a function with a homogeneous parameter pack. [[SO-1]] [[SO-2]] [[SO-3]] [[SO-4]] [[SO-5]] [[SO-6]] [[SO-7]] [[SO-8]]</p>

<p>In May 2022, a programmer asked for a fixed parameter type with ordinary implicit conversions and explained why constraining deduced argument types with <code>std::same_as</code> or <code>std::convertible_to</code> does not provide that behavior. [[SO-9]]
In January 2026, another programmer asked how to declare a function taking an even number of <code>int</code> arguments and was unsure why templates were needed when all parameters had a concrete type. [[SO-10]]</p>

<p>A constrained pack is the direct current-language alternative for a <code>min</code> interface comparing values of one type. The table also includes the recursive, initializer-list, and SFINAE approaches considered in earlier revisions. Each definition is an independent alternative.</p>
<table class=data>
<caption>Alternative min interfaces, including the proposed homogeneous-pack form.</caption>
    <tr>
        <th>Recursion</th>
        <td>
<xmp class=language-cpp>
template <class T>
T min(T v) {
    return v;
}

template <class T, class... Args>
T min(T v1, Args... vs) {
    T v2 = min(vs...);
    return v2 < v1 ? v2 : v1;
}
</xmp>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <th><code>std::initializer_list</code></th>
        <td>
<p>Precondition: <code>vs.size() &gt; 0</code>.</p>
<xmp class=language-cpp>
template <class T>
T min(std::initializer_list<T> vs) {
    auto i = vs.begin();
    T v = *i++;
    for (; i != vs.end(); ++i) {
        if (*i < v)
            v = *i;
    }

    return v;
}
</xmp>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <th>SFINAE</th>
        <td>
<xmp class=language-cpp>
template <
    class T, class... Args,
    std::enable_if_t<
        (... && std::is_same_v<Args, T>),
        std::nullptr_t> = nullptr>
T min(T v1, Args... vs) {
    (static_cast<void>(v1 = vs < v1 ? vs : v1), ...);
    return v1;
}
</xmp>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <th>Concepts</th>
        <td>
<xmp class=language-cpp>
template <class T, class... Args>
    requires (... && std::same_as<Args, T>)
T min(T v1, Args... vs) {
    (static_cast<void>(v1 = vs < v1 ? vs : v1), ...);
    return v1;
}
</xmp>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <th>Homogeneous pack (this proposal)</th>
        <td>
<xmp class=language-cpp>
template <class T>
T min(T v1, T... vs) {
    (static_cast<void>(v1 = vs < v1 ? vs : v1), ...);
    return v1;
}
</xmp>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<ul>
<li>The recursive interface appears to accept arguments of any type, but a non-<code>T</code> argument either fails to compile or requires a potentially unwanted implicit conversion.</li>
<li>The <code>std::initializer_list</code> interface states the accepted type, but limits mutability and requires braces around the arguments.
With an explicit argument before the list, <code>min</code>'s precondition could be removed, but calls would still need the form <code>min(a, { b, c, d })</code>.</li>
<li>The SFINAE constraint requires every argument to have the same type, but makes the interface harder to read. Using <code>is_same_v</code> with a non-dependent parameter type can also reject desired conversions.</li>
<li>The Concepts form enforces the same equal-type requirement as the SFINAE form without the <code>enable_if_t</code> template parameter.</li>
</ul>

<p>Explicitly specifying <code>T</code> also permits ordinary argument conversions in the proposed form. The SFINAE and Concepts forms still deduce each <code>Args</code> and reject a different type. For example, their <code>min&lt;int&gt;(1, 2.5)</code> calls fail the equal-type requirement. [[CXX-DEDUCTION-CALL]] [[CXX-EXPLICIT-ARGS]]</p>

<aside class=issue>
<p>Test the braced-initializer calls in [[#example-points]] in a compiler implementation. Compare constrained packs and initializer lists, covering deduction, empty calls and initializers, designated initializers, and narrowing. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/dcl.init.aggr">[dcl.init.aggr]</a> <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/temp.deduct.call">[temp.deduct.call]</a></p>
</aside>

<p>Replacing the library interfaces below with homogeneous packs would change their argument grouping or deduction. For example, the Library Fundamentals TS v2's <code>make_array</code> can find a common type for mixed arguments, whereas the proposed form deduces one <code>T</code> and rejects conflicting deductions. [[LFTS2-ARRAY-CREATION]] [[CXX-DEDUCTION-CALL]] [[CXX-MIN-MAX]] [[CXX-ARRAY-DEDUCTION]]</p>

<p>The library declarations below omit their enclosing namespace. The TS <code>make_array</code> declares an optional template parameter <code>D = void</code> for an explicitly supplied element type. Its return element type <code>VT</code> is <code>common_type_t&lt;Types...&gt;</code> when <code>D</code> is <code>void</code> and is <code>D</code> otherwise. The <code>std::array</code> deduction guide mandates <code>(is_same_v&lt;T, U&gt; &amp;&amp; ...)</code>. [[LFTS2-ARRAY-CREATION]] [[CXX-ARRAY-DEDUCTION]]</p>

<table class=data>
<caption>Library interfaces and proposed alternatives with different calling or deduction rules. Homogeneous deduction-guide support remains open.</caption>
    <tr><th>Current</th><th>With homogeneous packs</th></tr>
    <tr>
        <td>
<xmp class=language-cpp>
template <class T>
constexpr T min(initializer_list<T> t);

template <class T, class Compare>
constexpr T min(initializer_list<T> t, Compare comp);
</xmp>
        </td>
        <td>
<xmp class=language-cpp>
template <class T>
constexpr T min(T v1, T... vs);

template <class Compare, class T>
constexpr T min(Compare comp, T v1, T... vs);
</xmp>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td>
<xmp class=language-cpp>
template <class D = void, class... Types>
constexpr array<VT, sizeof...(Types)>
    make_array(Types&&... t);
</xmp>
        </td>
        <td>
<xmp class=language-cpp>
template <class T>
constexpr auto make_array(T... t)
    -> array<T, sizeof...(t)>;
</xmp>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td>
<xmp class=language-cpp>
template <class T, class... U>
array(T, U...) -> array<T, 1 + sizeof...(U)>;
</xmp>
        </td>
        <td>
<xmp class=language-cpp>
template <class T>
array(T... t) -> array<T, sizeof...(t)>;
</xmp>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<p>Explicitly supplying <code>T</code> in the proposed <code>make_array</code> permits ordinary parameter conversions and an empty pack, whereas an empty call cannot deduce <code>T</code>. The TS also permits an empty call when <code>D</code> is supplied. [[LFTS2-ARRAY-CREATION]] [[CXX-DEDUCTION-CALL]] [[CXX-EXPLICIT-ARGS]]</p>

<aside class=issue>
<p>Decide whether to support homogeneous packs in deduction guides and, if so, specify deduction and return-type rules.</p>
</aside>

## Initializer lists ## {#initializer-lists}

<table class=data>
<caption>Homogeneous function parameter packs compared with std::initializer_list.</caption>
    <tr><th></th><th><code>std::initializer_list</code></th><th>homogeneous packs</th></tr>
    <tr><th>Can the function be non-template?</th><td>Yes</td><td>No, even without a template head</td></tr>
    <tr><th>Can mutate elements?</th><td>The backing-array elements are const</td><td>Yes, when the declared element type permits it</td></tr>
    <tr><th>Can appear before other arguments?</th><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr>
    <tr><th>Get the number of elements</th><td><code class="prettyprint">x.size()</code></td><td><code class="prettyprint">sizeof...(x)</code></td></tr>
    <tr><th>Use the count in a return type or constraint?</th><td>No, the function parameter's value does not determine its function type or constraints</td><td>Yes, the count is fixed for each specialization</td></tr>
    <tr><th>Iterate over the elements</th><td>Range-based <code class="prettyprint">for</code></td><td>Fold expressions</td></tr>
</table>

<p><code>std::initializer_list::size()</code> is <code>constexpr</code>, so the size of a list usable in constant expressions can be computed at compile time. An ordinary initializer-list function parameter does not supply a constant-expression count for a return type or constraint, even when the caller passes a fixed number of elements. <code>sizeof...(x)</code> instead gives the specialization's pack length independently of the parameter values. [[CXX-INITIALIZER-LIST]] [[CXX-VARIADIC]]</p>

<p>A <code>std::initializer_list</code> provides access to an array of const elements and is passed as a single argument. Const reference wrappers still permit modification of the referenced objects: [[CXX-INITIALIZER-LIST]]</p>

<xmp class=language-cpp>
void translate(
    int dx, int dy,
    std::initializer_list<
        std::reference_wrapper<Point>> points) {
    for (Point& point : points) {
        point.x += dx;
        point.y += dy;
    }
}
</xmp>

<p>For the objects in [[#example-references]], <code>translate(2, 3, {start, finish})</code> makes the same coordinate changes. The wrappers group the references into one list argument, while either parameter-pack form accepts separate arguments. [[CXX-VARIADIC]]</p>

An initializer list also permits ownership transfer through user-defined wrappers with <code>mutable</code> members. Although the list elements are <code>const</code>, those members can be moved from: [[CXX-INITIALIZER-LIST]] [[CXX-MUTABLE]]

<xmp class=language-cpp>
template <class T>
struct MutableWrapper {
    mutable T value;
};

void enqueue_wrapped(
    std::vector<std::unique_ptr<Job>>& queue,
    std::initializer_list<
        MutableWrapper<std::unique_ptr<Job>>> jobs) {
    for (const auto& job : jobs) {
        queue.push_back(std::move(job.value));
    }
}

void submit_wrapped_jobs() {
    std::vector<std::unique_ptr<Job>> queue;
    auto first = std::make_unique<Job>(Job{1});

    enqueue_wrapped(
        queue,
        {{std::move(first)},
         {std::make_unique<Job>(Job{2})}});
}
</xmp>

The caller supplies the list and wrapper braces and the implementation consumes the mutable members before the backing array expires. Initializer-list copies share those members so consuming through one leaves moved-from members visible through the others. The function's interface still lacks a compile-time count. [[CXX-INITIALIZER-LIST]] [[CXX-LIST-INIT]]

<p>Initializer-list constructors have priority under [over.match.list]. A homogeneous-pack constructor would participate in ordinary overload selection instead so replacing the list constructor can change the selected overload. [[CXX-LIST-OVERLOAD]]</p>

<p>Consider a container that accepts its initial elements through an initializer-list constructor:</p>

<xmp class=language-cpp>
template <class T>
class MyContainer {
public:
    MyContainer(std::initializer_list<T> elems);
    // ...
};
</xmp>

<p>List-initialization selects this constructor:</p>

<xmp class=language-cpp>
// invokes MyContainer<int>::MyContainer(std::initializer_list<int>)
MyContainer<int> cont = {5, 10};
</xmp>

<p>If homogeneous packs are permitted in constructors, the corresponding constructor would permit the same initialization:</p>

<aside class=issue>
<p>Decide whether to permit homogeneous packs in constructors and test the examples under R2's overload model, treating the constructor as an implicit template.</p>
</aside>

<xmp class=language-cpp>
template <class T>
class MyContainer {
public:
    MyContainer(const T&... elems);
    // ...
};

// invokes MyContainer<int>::MyContainer(const int&, const int&)
MyContainer<int> cont = {5, 10};
</xmp>

<p>Adding a constructor for a minimum and maximum changes which overload is selected for two elements:</p>

<xmp class=language-cpp>
template <class T>
class MyContainer {
public:
    MyContainer(const T&... elems);
    explicit MyContainer(const T& min, const T& max);
    // ...
};

// error: MyContainer<int>::MyContainer(const int&, const int&) is explicit
MyContainer<int> cont = {5, 10};
</xmp>

<p>For exactly two elements, the additional constructor prevents selection of the homogeneous constructor regardless of initialization syntax. In the initializer-list version shown in the table below, list-initialization selects the <code>std::initializer_list</code> constructor. Direct non-list initialization can select or avoid that constructor unambiguously.</p>

<table class="data constructor-comparison">
<caption>Constructor selection for the listed declarations. The homogeneous-pack column gives proposed outcomes.</caption>
<colgroup><col style="width: 20%"><col style="width: 40%"><col style="width: 40%"></colgroup>
<thead>
    <tr><th scope=col>Declaration</th><th scope=col>With <code>std::initializer_list</code></th><th scope=col>With homogeneous packs</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
    <tr>
        <td></td>
        <td><xmp class=language-cpp>
template <class T>
class MyContainer {
public:
    MyContainer(
        std::initializer_list<T> elems);
    explicit MyContainer(
        const T& min, const T& max);
    // ...
};
</xmp>
        </td>
        <td><xmp class=language-cpp>
template <class T>
class MyContainer {
public:
    MyContainer(
        const T&... elems);
    explicit MyContainer(
        const T& min, const T& max);
    // ...
};
</xmp>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><code class="prettyprint">MyContainer&lt;int&gt; cont{1, 2, 3};</code></td>
        <td>Selects <code>std::initializer_list</code> constructor</td>
        <td>Selects homogeneous pack constructor</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><code class="prettyprint">MyContainer&lt;int&gt; cont = { 1, 2, 3 };</code></td>
        <td>Selects <code>std::initializer_list</code> constructor</td>
        <td>Selects homogeneous pack constructor</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><code class="prettyprint">MyContainer&lt;int&gt; cont(1, 2, 3);</code></td>
        <td>Error: no matching constructor</td>
        <td>Selects homogeneous pack constructor</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><code class="prettyprint">MyContainer&lt;int&gt; cont({ 1, 2, 3 });</code></td>
        <td>Selects <code>std::initializer_list</code> constructor</td>
        <td>Selects implicit move constructor, initializing its temporary argument through the homogeneous pack constructor</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><code class="prettyprint">MyContainer&lt;int&gt; cont{1, 2};</code></td>
        <td>Selects <code>std::initializer_list</code> constructor</td>
        <td>Selects <code>explicit</code> constructor</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><code class="prettyprint">MyContainer&lt;int&gt; cont = { 1, 2 };</code></td>
        <td>Selects <code>std::initializer_list</code> constructor</td>
        <td>Error: selects <code>explicit</code> constructor</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><code class="prettyprint">MyContainer&lt;int&gt; cont(1, 2);</code></td>
        <td>Selects <code>explicit</code> constructor</td>
        <td>Selects <code>explicit</code> constructor</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><code class="prettyprint">MyContainer&lt;int&gt; cont({ 1, 2 });</code></td>
        <td>Selects <code>std::initializer_list</code> constructor</td>
        <td>Error: nested copy-list-initialization selects the <code>explicit</code> two-argument constructor</td>
    </tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p>In the parenthesized calls with a braced argument, that argument can initialize a temporary <code>MyContainer&lt;int&gt;</code> for the implicit move constructor. Copy-list-initialization of the temporary permits the homogeneous constructor for three elements but rejects the selected <code>explicit</code> constructor for two. [[CXX-LIST-CONVERSION]]</p>

<aside class=issue>
<p>Agree with Jan and Eczbek which maintained interface to use as an example. Compare homogeneous packs with constrained packs, initializer lists, ranges, and bounded overloads.</p>
</aside>

