# Design Decisions # {#design-decisions}

## Definitions ## {#definitions}

A <em>homogeneous parameter pack</em> (or <em>homogeneous pack</em>) contains values of one type and its declaration introduces an independent count. A <em>heterogeneous parameter pack</em> (or <em>heterogeneous pack</em>) permits values of different types. Both terms apply to function parameter packs and template parameter packs.

A function parameter pack's <em>parameter pattern</em> is its parameter declaration with the declarator ellipsis removed. For an <code>auto...</code> parameter, ordinary placeholder processing first invents a type parameter pack. [[CXX-FUNCTIONS]] A declaration is homogeneous only if its pattern contains no unexpanded pack after that processing. An ordinary expansion remains an ordinary expansion even when every resulting parameter type is the same.

## Pack declarations ## {#implicit-template}

The same declarator syntax can express either an ordinary expansion or a new homogeneous pack:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
// heterogeneous function parameter pack
template <class... T>
void f(T... v);

// homogeneous function parameter pack
template <class T>
void f(T... v);
</xmp>

The pattern need not mention a template parameter:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
// homogeneous function parameter pack
void f(int... v);
</xmp>

A homogeneous function parameter pack makes the declaration an abbreviated function template. Its signature establishes that status before the body is parsed.

P1061R10 section 3.3 removed support for structured binding packs outside templates after objections to R9's implicit template region, which covered the remainder of an ordinary function after a structured binding declaration. Here the signature declares the whole function a template. This distinction does not establish committee agreement on the proposal. [[P1061R10]]

In a function call, the pack's size is deduced from the arguments. It must therefore appear in a deduced context, limiting a function template to one homogeneous pack at the end of its parameter list, with no following C ellipsis. Once its parameters are formed, the pack behaves like any other function parameter pack.

### Abbreviated syntax ### {#abbreviated-syntax}

The February 2019 Kona EWGI poll in [[#straw-polls]] records support for R2's named form, not acceptance of this revision or its wording. [[P1219R2]] [[N4795]] Since C++20, a function template that needs no template parameter names can use abbreviated syntax, with placeholders such as <code>auto</code> in its parameter list. [[P1141R2]] [[CXX-FUNCTIONS]]

The proposed counterpart is:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
void foo(int... v);
</xmp>

If the homogeneous pack is the only reason the declaration is a template and its pattern contains no dependent names, the template has no formal template parameters. The <code nohighlight>template-head</code> must then be omitted. An empty head would be unambiguous here, but the proposal disallows it on template declarations to avoid confusion with explicit specializations:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
template <class T>
void foo(T... v); // #1 OK

// #2 OK, declares a template with an empty template parameter list
void foo(int... v);

// Error: function parameter pack in explicit specialization
template <>
void foo(float... v);

// OK, declares an explicit specialization of #2
template <>
void foo(int a);

// OK, declares an explicit specialization of #2
template <>
void foo<>(int a, int b);

// OK, declares an explicit specialization of #1
template <>
void foo<int>(int a, int b);

// OK, declares an explicit specialization of #1
template <>
void foo(float a);
</xmp>

The difference between deducing element types and fixing one type is visible in these declarations:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
void a(auto... values) {}
void b(int... values) {}
</xmp>

The first already declares an abbreviated function template in C++26 and permits different deduced argument types. The second is ill-formed in C++26 and would declare a homogeneous pack with a fixed element type and an independently deduced count under this proposal. [[CXX-FUNCTIONS]] [[CXX-VARIADIC]]

The function template, its ordinary template arguments, and the pack length determine the specialization. Different counts identify different specializations. Converting arguments to a fixed element type does not add their original types to the specialization's identity. These proposed uses therefore select the same two-parameter specialization:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
void use_b() {
    b(1, 2);
    b(1.0, 2.0);
    void (*callback)(int, int) = b;
    callback(3, 4);
}
</xmp>

For the count two, <code>b</code> has the ordinary function type <code>void(int, int)</code>. Function-type equality alone does not identify a specialization: different ordinary template arguments can produce the same adjusted function type. [[CXX-DEDUCTION-GENERAL]]

The programmer cannot name or explicitly supply the implicit count as a constant template parameter. An empty template-argument list does not specify the count either so it must still be deduced. The count does not make every expression in the function body dependent.

### Type deduction ### {#deduction}

In the proposed <code>sum</code>, the element type is fixed as <code>int</code>. Calls deduce only the pack length and initialize each parameter using ordinary conversions:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
int sum(int... values) {
    return (0 + ... + values);
}
</xmp>

<table class=data>
<caption>Proposed calls to <code>sum</code>: fixed <code>int</code> element type.</caption>
<thead><tr><th scope=col>Call</th><th scope=col>Proposed outcome</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
  <tr><td><code>sum()</code></td><td>Length zero. The initialized addition fold returns zero.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>sum(1, 2.5)</code></td><td>Length two. Ordinary conversion initializes both <code>int</code> parameters.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

For the proposed <code>total</code> without an explicit template argument, <code>T</code> is deduced from every applicable argument. This deduction neither chooses the first argument's type nor computes a common type. [[CXX-DEDUCTION-CALL]]

<xmp class=language-cpp>
template <class T>
T total(T... values) {
    return (T{} + ... + values);
}
</xmp>

<table class=data>
<caption>Proposed calls to <code>total</code> with <code>T</code> deduced.</caption>
<thead><tr><th scope=col>Call</th><th scope=col>Proposed outcome</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
  <tr><td><code>total(1, 2)</code></td><td>Deduce <code>T</code> as <code>int</code> and length two.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>total(1, 2.5)</code></td><td>Reject conflicting deductions for <code>T</code>.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>total()</code></td><td>Reject because there is no argument from which to deduce <code>T</code>.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

With <code>total&lt;int&gt;</code>, the caller supplies <code>T</code> explicitly. Ordinary conversions initialize the <code>int</code> parameters and the pack length is still deduced from the arguments. [[CXX-EXPLICIT-ARGS]]

<table class=data>
<caption>Proposed calls to <code>total&lt;int&gt;</code>: explicitly supplied element type.</caption>
<thead><tr><th scope=col>Call</th><th scope=col>Proposed outcome</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
  <tr><td><code>total&lt;int&gt;(1, 2.5)</code></td><td>Use the explicit element type and ordinary conversions.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>total&lt;int&gt;()</code></td><td>Length zero with a known element type.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

A zero count makes no deductions for template parameters. An element type must still be supplied explicitly, deduced elsewhere, or obtained from a default. Substitution into the element pattern follows the ordinary substitution rules even when the count is zero.

Ordinary reference binding, list-initialization, parameter adjustment, and lifetime rules apply. A braced argument initializes its corresponding parameter and retains narrowing checks. A temporary bound to a reference parameter persists until completion of the full-expression containing the call. Declaring a pack neither extends that lifetime nor imposes a new evaluation order on argument initialization. [[CXX-FUNCTIONS]] [[CXX-LIST-INIT]] [[CXX-TEMPORARIES]] [[CXX-CALL]]

<aside class=issue>
<p>Test deduction and initialization in a compiler implementation with move-only arguments, forwarding references, overloaded function names, braced arguments, constraints, exception specifications, and constant evaluation.</p>
</aside>

### Address-taking ### {#addresses}

For the <code>sum</code> and <code>total</code> declarations above, taking an address deduces the count from the target function type. Explicit specialization and instantiation declarations use the same deduction. Each row below is independent.

<table class=data>
<caption>Proposed address-taking and declaration behavior. The element type and count both need to match the identified function. The unnamed declaration depends on approval to reclaim the comma-less spelling.</caption>
<thead><tr><th scope=col>Operation</th><th scope=col>Proposed outcome</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
  <tr><td><code>int (*zero)() = sum</code></td><td>Select length zero.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>int (*two)(int, int) = sum</code></td><td>Select length two.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>auto two = &amp;sum</code> or <code>&amp;sum&lt;&gt;</code></td><td>Reject because no length is determined.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>auto two = &amp;total&lt;int&gt;</code></td><td>Reject because specifying <code>T</code> does not supply the length.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>int (*two)(double, double) = sum</code></td><td>Reject the mismatched parameter types. Call conversions do not adapt a function pointer signature.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>int sum(int...)</code> and a named definition</td><td>Corresponding declarations of the same template.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>int sum(int, int)</code></td><td>An ordinary overload, not an implicit specialization declaration.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

The existing explicit-specialization and explicit-instantiation syntax is retained. [[CXX-SPECIALIZATION]] [[CXX-INSTANTIATION]] In these proposed declarations, the ordinary parameter list identifies the count:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
template <>
int sum<>(int value) {
    return value + 10;
}

template int sum<>(int, int);
</xmp>

Ordinary target-type matching applies after count deduction.

<aside class=issue>
<p>Specify count deduction and call-operator selection for generic-lambda conversion to a function pointer. A typed target should work, while unary plus should fail when the count is undetermined. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/expr.prim.lambda.closure">[expr.prim.lambda.closure]</a></p>
</aside>

The count identifies the same specialization whether the function is called or its address is taken. A runtime variadic interface would instead need a representation of the arguments and their count at runtime, which would not provide <code>sizeof...</code> for return types or constraints.

### Overload ordering ### {#overload-ordering}

The proposed ordering policy retains ordinary conversion ranking, partial ordering, and constraint subsumption. Homogeneity introduces no additional preference. Partial ordering compares template declarations while retaining which parameters came from packs, rather than comparing only the resulting fixed-arity specializations. [[CXX-OVERLOAD]] [[CXX-PARTIAL-ORDER]] [[CXX-FUNCTION-ORDER]]

A call with no arguments for a pack contributes no element-type comparisons to partial ordering. A default template argument can make a candidate viable, but does not turn its dependent pattern into a fixed type for ordering. A non-template overload wins a tie against a template specialization under the existing rules. A fixed-arity template still participates in template partial ordering.

<table class=data>
<caption>Proposed overload outcomes under the ordinary-ordering policy. Each row is independent.</caption>
<thead><tr><th scope=col>Overloads</th><th scope=col>Calls and proposed outcomes</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><code>void fixed(int... values);</code><br>
<code>template &lt;class T&gt; void fixed(T... values);</code></td>
<td><code>fixed(1, 2)</code> selects the fixed-element template. <code>fixed(1.0, 2.0)</code> selects the deduced-element template by better conversions. <code>fixed()</code> selects the fixed-element template because the other cannot deduce <code>T</code>.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>template &lt;class T&gt; void packed(T... values);</code><br>
<code>template &lt;class... Ts&gt; void packed(Ts... values);</code></td>
<td><code>packed(1, 2)</code> is ambiguous. <code>packed(1, 2.0)</code> and <code>packed()</code> select the heterogeneous template because the homogeneous candidate cannot deduce a consistent <code>T</code>.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>void empty(int... values);</code><br>
<code>void empty(double... values);</code></td>
<td><code>empty()</code> is ambiguous. The zero count supplies no element-type comparison.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>void pair(int, int);</code><br>
<code>void pair(int... values);</code></td>
<td><code>pair(1, 2)</code> selects the non-template overload after tied conversions.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>template &lt;class T&gt; void paired(T, T);</code><br>
<code>template &lt;class T&gt; void paired(T... values);</code></td>
<td><code>paired(1, 2)</code> selects the fixed-arity template under ordinary template partial ordering.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>template &lt;class T&gt; void single(T);</code><br>
<code>void single(int... values);</code></td>
<td><code>single(1)</code> is ambiguous. The fixed-arity pattern and fixed element type do not establish one template as more specialized.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

The implicit count is determined before evaluating an associated constraint that uses it. Separate predicates such as <code>sizeof...(values) &gt;= 1</code> and <code>sizeof...(values) &gt;= 2</code> gain no arithmetic implication rule. [[CXX-CONSTRAINT-ORDER]]

<aside class=issue>
<p>Specify correspondence for count expressions in atomic constraints and fold-expanded constraints. The implicit count is not a template parameter, so those rules need an explicit audit rather than an assumed mapping to an invented parameter.</p>
</aside>

## Lambdas ## {#lambdas}

A homogeneous function parameter pack makes a lambda generic even when its element type is fixed, as in this product of <code>int</code> parameters:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
auto a = [](int... v) {
    return (1 * ... * v);
};
</xmp>

An ordinary expansion of an enclosing pack does not by itself make a lambda generic, including when the element type uses pack indexing. [[CXX-LAMBDA]]

## Pack indexing ## {#indexing}

For a type pack <code>Ts</code>, <code>Ts...[1]</code> selects its second type, which can serve as the proposed homogeneous pack's element type. In the examples below, <code>Is</code> is a pack of indices. The expansion pattern of pack indexing is the pack name alone. In <code>Ts...[Is]</code>, the indexed occurrence of <code>Ts</code> belongs to that expansion while <code>Is</code> remains available to an enclosing expansion. These C++26 rules apply to the proposed declarations as well. [[CXX-VARIADIC]] [[CXX-PACK-INDEX]]

The following is valid C++26. Each lambda has one unnamed parameter:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
template <class... Ts, std::size_t... Is>
void indexed(std::index_sequence<Is...>) {
    (..., [](Ts...[Is]) {});
}

int main() {
    indexed<int, double, char>(
        std::index_sequence<2, 0, 2, 1>{});
}
</xmp>

The four indices select <code>char</code>, <code>int</code>, <code>char</code>, and <code>double</code> from the three types. Each indexed type is replaced in full by the selected type, with no ellipsis or bracket suffix left over.

Each row below is independent and assumes an appropriate enclosing declaration of <code>Ts</code> and <code>Is</code>, with valid selected indices. Only the unnamed homogeneous form requires the comma-less varargs change in [[#compatibility]].

<table class=data>
<caption>C++26 and proposed interpretations of indexed lambda parameters and enclosing folds.</caption>
<thead><tr><th scope=col>Spelling</th><th scope=col>C++26 interpretation</th><th scope=col>Proposed interpretation</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
  <tr><td><code>[](Ts...[1]) {}</code></td><td>One unnamed parameter of the selected type.</td><td>Unchanged.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>(..., [](Ts...[1]) {})</code></td><td>Ill-formed. No unexpanded pack remains for the fold.</td><td>Unchanged.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>(..., [](Ts...[Is]) {})</code></td><td>The fold expands <code>Is</code> into single-parameter lambdas.</td><td>Unchanged.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>(..., [] { Ts...[Is]{}; })</code></td><td>The fold expands <code>Is</code>. Constructing these lambdas does not invoke their bodies.</td><td>Unchanged.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>[](Ts...[1]... values) {}</code></td><td>Ill-formed. The parameter declaration has no unexpanded pack.</td><td>A homogeneous pack of the selected type with its own count.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>[](Ts...[Is]... values) {}</code></td><td>The parameter declaration expands <code>Is</code> into one parameter list.</td><td>Unchanged. No new count.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>(..., [](Ts...[Is]... values) {})</code></td><td>Ill-formed. The inner declaration already expands <code>Is</code>.</td><td>Unchanged.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>[](Ts... values) {}</code></td><td>Ordinary expansion of the enclosing <code>Ts</code>. This alone does not make the lambda generic.</td><td>Unchanged.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>[](int... values) {}</code></td><td>Ill-formed.</td><td>A generic lambda with one homogeneous pack.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>[](int...) {}</code></td><td>One <code>int</code> parameter followed by C varargs.</td><td>An unnamed homogeneous pack. Compatibility change.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>[](int, ...) {}</code></td><td>One <code>int</code> parameter followed by C varargs.</td><td>Unchanged.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

The varying-index form with a second ellipsis leaves no unexpanded pack for an enclosing fold, because the parameter declaration already expands <code>Is</code>. [[CXX-FOLDS]] [[CXX-VARIADIC]]

<aside class=issue>
<p>Confirm whether C++26 [dcl.fct] treats unnamed <code>Ts...[1]...</code> as C varargs after indexing expands <code>Ts</code>. Coordinate the transition with Jan. <a href="https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n5050/dcl.fct">[dcl.fct]</a></p>
</aside>

### Pairwise and Cartesian expansion ### {#multiple-packs}

Pairwise expansion places both packs in one pattern and requires equal lengths. This current-language fragment pairs each type with one index constant:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
(..., [](Ts, std::integral_constant<std::size_t, Is>) {});
</xmp>

This does not index <code>Ts</code>. By contrast, a Cartesian product requires separate nested expansions. This current-language fragment fixes one type before expanding all index constants:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
template <class... Ts, std::size_t... Is>
void cartesian(std::index_sequence<Is...> indices) {
    (..., []<class T>(std::index_sequence<Is...>) {
        (..., [](T, std::integral_constant<std::size_t, Is>) {});
    }.template operator()<Ts>(indices));
}
</xmp>

With three types and four index entries, the nested expansions form twelve pairings. Equal-length packs or repeated identical types can conceal the distinction between selection and pairing. The existing equal-length requirement and innermost-expansion rule account for each operation without changing the meaning of indexing. [[CXX-VARIADIC]]

### Homogeneous packs of indexed types ### {#indexed-homogeneous}

A second declarator ellipsis can introduce the proposed homogeneous pack when the index is a scalar. The indexed type is selected before the new pack's independent count is deduced:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
template <class... Ts>
auto selected_counter() {
    return [](Ts...[1]... values) {
        return sizeof...(values);
    };
}
</xmp>

For <code>selected_counter&lt;int, double, char&gt;()</code>, the proposed lambda accepts a deduced number of ordinary <code>double</code> parameters. That count is unrelated to the length of <code>Ts</code>.

To create homogeneous lambdas selected by different indices, pass each index to a helper as a scalar. The following example uses the proposed syntax:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
template <class... Ts>
struct selectors {
    template <std::size_t I>
    static auto make() {
        return [](Ts...[I]... values) {
            return sizeof...(values);
        };
    }
};

template <class... Ts, std::size_t... Is>
auto make_selected(std::index_sequence<Is...>) {
    return std::tuple{selectors<Ts...>::template make<Is>()...};
}
</xmp>

Each resulting call operator has its own homogeneous count. The expansion of <code>Is</code> in <code>make_selected</code> supplies a scalar index to each specialization of <code>make</code>.

### Array parameters ### {#arrays}

In C++26, the named declaration <code>int... values[Is]</code> expands <code>Is</code> into array bounds. The resulting array parameters undergo ordinary pointer adjustment. With positive extents 1, 2, and 3, the resulting function type has three <code>int*</code> parameters. Zero extents are independently invalid. [[CXX-FUNCTIONS]] [[CXX-ARRAYS]]

The C++26 [dcl.name] grammar has no array-suffix production for an abstract (unnamed) pack declarator. P2662R3 removed that production when introducing pack indexing. The named array declaration remains valid. [[CXX-DECLARATORS]] [[P2662R3]]

An enclosing fold around the named array-pack declaration has no unexpanded <code>Is</code> left because the parameter declaration already expands it.

The proposal retains the C++26 abstract-declarator grammar. If the committee approves reclamation, an array alias could supply an unnamed homogeneous parameter type with ordinary adjustment. Its array extent remains separate from the homogeneous count.

### Empty packs and return types ### {#empty-and-return}

An empty outer expansion over <code>Is</code> need not instantiate an indexed selection. A fixed index still needs to designate an element when its type is required. In the proposed homogeneous form, calling a lambda with zero arguments does not repair an invalid fixed selection in its declaration. [[CXX-VARIADIC]] [[CXX-PACK-INDEX]]

The lambda body <code>{ Ts...[Is]{}; }</code> in the indexing table discards the constructed value and returns <code>void</code>. For the scalar types above, replacing it with <code>{ return Ts...[Is]{}; }</code> deduces the selected scalar type under the C++26 return-deduction rules. [[CXX-LAMBDA]] [[CXX-AUTO]]

## Captures ## {#captures}

Constant template parameters can supply values inside a lambda without capturing automatic function parameter objects. This current-language program prints <code>abc</code> without a newline:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
template <char... C, std::size_t... I>
void print_constants(std::index_sequence<I...>) {
    (..., [] {
        std::putchar(C...[I]);
    }());
}

int main() {
    print_constants<'a', 'b', 'c'>(
        std::make_index_sequence<3>());
}
</xmp>

The fold expands <code>I</code> and value pack indexing selects from <code>C</code>. Repeated indices 2, 0, 2, and 1 produce <code>cacb</code>. Homogeneous packs leave this program unchanged. [[CXX-VALUE-PACK-INDEX]] [[CXX-VARIADIC]]

In the proposed counterpart, <code>characters</code> names function parameter objects:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
void print_characters(char... characters) {
    (..., [&] {
        std::putchar(characters);
    }());
}
</xmp>

These function parameter objects require capture. Value capture follows the ordinary copying rules. [[CXX-CAPTURES]]

The lambda can be omitted:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
(..., std::putchar(characters));
</xmp>

Nested lambdas retain ordinary capture propagation. Reference capture does not extend a parameter's lifetime. [[CXX-CAPTURES]]

## Alternatives ## {#alternatives}

None of the designs in this section is adopted by this revision. References to proposed behavior in their examples describe the alternative being discussed.

### An additional auto marker ### {#additional-auto}

An alternative spelling <code>int auto... values</code> could mark the declaration as a template while keeping a fixed element type. It would require new grammar and an exception to the ordinary role of <code>auto</code> as a placeholder. A type name such as <code>int</code> is not a concept constraint. [[CXX-AUTO]]

Homogeneous template parameter packs already use <code>std::size_t... values</code>. Requiring <code>std::size_t auto... values</code> only for function parameters would give the two homogeneous declarations different spellings. This revision retains the unambiguous named form <code>T... values</code>. Its declarator ellipsis establishes template status.

### Empty template heads ### {#empty-template-heads}

An alternative is to allow empty template parameter lists in template declarations:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
// OK, declares a template with a homogeneous parameter pack
template <>
void foo(float... v);
</xmp>

Although this alternative is syntactically unambiguous, its resemblance to explicit specializations could confuse programmers. In an e-mail after the 2018 San Diego meeting, Richard Smith said he would oppose adopting homogeneous function parameter packs with this syntax. EWGI shared that view in the 2019 Kona poll recorded in [[#straw-polls]]. [[P1219R2]]

Combining empty heads on template declarations with omission of <code>template &lt;&gt;</code> on explicit specializations could reduce their resemblance and make template and non-template declarations more consistent.

### Alternative specialization syntax ### {#alternative-specializations}

The <code>template &lt;&gt;</code> prefix makes an explicit specialization look like a template declaration. The function, class, and variable specializations considered here are ordinary non-template entities. A <code nohighlight>template-id</code> can identify the specialization named in or deducible from the declaration, making the introducer redundant. [[CXX-SPECIALIZATION]]

This revision retains the existing explicit-specialization syntax. Under the alternative below, declarations could omit the <code>template &lt;&gt;</code> introducer without reducing expressivity:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
// class template
template <class T>
class C;

// ok, explicit specialization (proposed: not deprecated)
template <>
class C<int>;

// proposed: explicit specialization
class C<int>;

// variable template
template <int V>
constexpr int v = V;

// ok, explicit specialization (proposed: deprecated)
template <>
constexpr int v<1> = 0;

// proposed: explicit specialization
constexpr int v<1> = 0;

// function template
template <class T, class U>
void f(T t, U u);

// ok: explicit specialization (proposed: deprecated)
template <>
void f<int, double>(int i, double d);

// ok: explicit specialization (proposed: deprecated)
template <>
void f<>(int i, double d);

// ok: explicit specialization (proposed: deprecated)
template <>
void f(int i, double d);

// proposed: explicit specialization
void f<int, double>(int i, double d);

// proposed: explicit specialization
void f<>(int i, double d);

// ok: ordinary function declaration (proposed: no change)
void f(int i, double d);
</xmp>

The spellings are alternatives. The two definitions of <code>v&lt;1&gt;</code> cannot appear together.

R2 assessed this alternative as follows in 2019: [[P1219R2]]

<blockquote>
<p>Under this approach, the misleading part of the syntax is removed, and the result is a more consistent language. Non-template entities no longer look like templates. For explicit specializations of function and variable templates, the old syntax can be deprecated. (The old syntax for explicit specializations of class templates probably cannot be deprecated at this time, as their use is <em>very</em> common.) Anecdotally, explicit specializations of function templates are rare and their use is generally discouraged due to subtleties around overloading.</p>
</blockquote>

EWGI in Kona in 2019 did not poll this option. R2 reports sentiment in favor of a separate paper exploring it. [[P1219R2]]

### Explicit template markers ### {#marker}

An alternative is to require a marker in the template parameter list for declarations with a homogeneous pack:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
template <...>
void foo(int... v);

template <class T, ...>
void bar(T... v);
</xmp>

R2 reported the following implementation experience and discussions after the San Diego meeting in 2018: [[P1219R2]]

<blockquote>
<p>This idea, including the use of the ellipsis as the sigil, was suggested by Richard Smith in an impromptu conversation during the San Diego meeting. He and David Vandevoorde both indicated that this requirement would make the feature simpler to implement across a broad range of compilers. This notion is not directly supported by the experience of implementing the feature in Clang, but there is as yet no implementation experience with any other compiler. In Clang, the presence of an unparenthesized ellipsis in a declarator causes the compiler to treat the declaration as having a particular dependent type corresponding to a parameter pack, and the presence of a parameter of that type is enough to differentiate a function declaration as a template declaration instead of an explicit specialization or ordinary function declaration.</p>

<p>P1141R2, adopted in San Diego, adds support for function template declarations that lack a template head entirely; in this case, the presence of the <code>auto</code> keyword in the function parameter list indicates that the function declaration declares a function template. Any implementation that can handle these new declarations should also be able to handle homogeneous packs without added syntax. In a follow-up conversation with Richard over e-mail, he agreed with this conclusion. [[P1141R2]]</p>
</blockquote>

The corresponding lambda form would be <code>[]&lt;...&gt;(int... values) {}</code>. A marker could declare the independent count and separately opt into revised unnamed-ellipsis parsing. Making a lambda generic alone does not change indexing or move <code>Is</code> between expansions.

This revision retains abbreviated declarations without an explicit marker. A marker would make template status visible before the parameter list, but it would not replace the rules for count deduction, specialization identity, or overload ordering. Adopting it would require separate grammar and an explicit decision about whether it changes unnamed-ellipsis parsing.

### Bare template introducer ### {#bare-template-introducer}

An alternative omits the angle brackets when no template parameters are needed:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
template void foo(int... v);
</xmp>

This form resembles an explicit instantiation, which uses <code>template</code> without angle brackets but does not declare a template. [[CXX-INSTANTIATION]] Like the empty-head alternative, it remains unambiguous because the homogeneous pack identifies a template. R2 rejected this approach because a template declaration introduced by <code>template</code> requires an angle-bracketed parameter list. [[P1219R2]]

### Explicit lengths ### {#explicit-lengths}

An alternative is to allow or require a template parameter for the size of the pack. The spelling considered in R2 predates pack indexing: [[P1219R2]]

<xmp class=language-cpp>
template <std::size_t Len>
void foo(int...[Len] v);
</xmp>

A formal length parameter would allow callers to specify the pack length and make its role in specialization explicit. The proposed implicit count can only be deduced.

The language permits querying a pack's size with <code>sizeof...</code>, but not specifying it explicitly. Explicit lengths could apply to homogeneous template parameter packs as well as homogeneous function parameter packs. Keeping lengths optional in both forms would preserve the existing template-pack spelling and give the two forms a consistent interface.

A type-pack variant spelled <code>Ts...[Len]</code> conflicts with C++26 indexed type selection: that spelling already selects one type from <code>Ts</code>. An explicit marker does not resolve that conflict by itself.

Constraints on <code>sizeof...</code> can restrict the implicit count. Explicit lengths, multiple homogeneous packs, and non-trailing homogeneous packs are deferred. Restoring removed abstract array-pack grammar would be a separate change.

### Trivial templates ### {#trivial-templates}

Extending the empty-template-head alternative beyond homogeneous packs would also permit templates with no template parameters and no homogeneous pack:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
template <>
void foo(int n);
</xmp>

The empty-template-head alternative makes the <code nohighlight>template-parameter-list</code> optional in a <code nohighlight>template-head</code> to permit homogeneous packs with non-dependent types. It still requires at least one template parameter when there is no homogeneous pack. Relaxing that requirement would allow trivial templates with no template arguments or homogeneous pack count to vary between specializations. These would differ little from ordinary non-template entities, though a trivial function template could be defined in a header without <code>inline</code>.

This alternative also creates an ambiguity with explicit specializations:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
// #1: trivial function template
template <>
void f(int v);

// #2: function template with homogeneous pack
template <>
void f(int... vs);

// #3: redeclaration of #1 or explicit specialization of #2?
template <>
void f(int v);
</xmp>

Apart from minor simplifications in the language specification, this alternative offers little beyond making <code>[]&lt;&gt;(){}</code> well-formed, while introducing the specialization ambiguity above.

# Reclaiming the deprecated comma-less spelling # {#compatibility}

We ask the committee to build on P3176R1 by reclaiming the deprecated comma-less spelling so homogeneous packs can use existing pack syntax with optional parameter names. Jan Schultke's P3176R1, <em>The Oxford variadic comma</em>, continued the comma-separation work from P1219R2. It deprecated the comma-less spelling while retaining its meaning and standalone ellipsis parameters. [[P3176R1]] [[P1219R2]] [[CXX-VARARGS-DEPRECATION]]

If the committee approves reclamation, <code>void send(int... values)</code> and <code>void send(int...)</code> would declare the same function template. C varargs would retain the distinct spelling <code>void send(int, ...)</code>.

Requiring pack names would avoid reclamation but add otherwise unnecessary names to declarations. An explicit marker ([[#marker]]) could preserve optional names at the cost of extra syntax. A different spelling could avoid reuse but would depart from existing pack syntax.

## Current rules and history ## {#current-comma-rules}

The February 2019 Kona EWGI poll in [[#straw-polls]] supported making the varargs comma mandatory. [[P1219R2]] [[N4795]]

A <em>varargs ellipsis</em> is a C-style ellipsis rather than a parameter-pack ellipsis. C requires an intervening comma when a parameter list precedes it. C23 also permits an ellipsis alone: [[N3096]]

<pre nohighlight>
<code nohighlight>parameter-type-list:</code>
    <code nohighlight>parameter-list</code>
    <code nohighlight>parameter-list , ...</code>
    <code nohighlight>...</code>
</pre>

C++26 also permits a standalone varargs ellipsis. Unlike C, it additionally permits a varargs ellipsis after a parameter list without an intervening comma. The comma-less alternative is deprecated in C++26: [[CXX-FUNCTIONS]] [[CXX-VARARGS-DEPRECATION]]

<pre nohighlight>
<code nohighlight>parameter-declaration-clause:</code>
    <code nohighlight>...</code>
    <code nohighlight>parameter-declaration-list</code><sub>opt</sub>
    <code nohighlight>parameter-declaration-list , ...</code>
    <code nohighlight>parameter-declaration-list ...</code>
</pre>

C++ introduced varargs ellipses with function prototypes and initially prohibited a comma before the ellipsis. C later adopted both features but required a comma to distinguish the last formal parameter from varargs. C++ then permitted the comma for C compatibility, leaving it optional. [[P1219R2]]

An ellipsis at the end of a function parameter list that could be part of an abstract (nameless) declarator declares a pack if the parameter's type names an unexpanded template parameter pack or contains <code>auto</code>. Otherwise, it is a varargs ellipsis. [[CXX-FUNCTIONS]]

For example, these declarations have different meanings in C++26:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
// declares a variadic function template with a function parameter pack
template <class... T>
void f(T...);

// same as void f(T, ...)
template <class T>
void f(T...);
</xmp>

## Proposed change ## {#proposed-comma-change}

Under the proposed rules, the second declaration would declare a homogeneous function parameter pack. Requiring a comma between a parameter list and a varargs ellipsis would make the disambiguation rule unnecessary while preserving C-compatible declarations.

Reusing the spelling would be a breaking change to source and link compatibility under SD-10. [[SD10]] The current declaration <code>void send(int...)</code> takes one <code>int</code> followed by C varargs. Its proposed homogeneous meaning would change accepted calls, initialization, function types, address-taking, and template linkage consequences. The explicit comma spelling <code>void send(int, ...)</code> retains its meaning. [[CXX-FUNCTIONS]]

R2 made the following migration argument in 2019: [[P1219R2]]

<blockquote>
<p>This is a breaking change, but likely not a very impactful one. In the parlance of P0684R2, this would be a "Very Good Change": Compilers can issue warnings in C++20 mode (or earlier) whenever the disambiguation rule is resolved in favor of a varargs ellipsis, and the warning can be resolved without any change in meaning by the addition of a single character. Moreover, the number of instances should be vanishingly small. In modern C++ code, the varargs ellipsis has largely been superseded by function parameter packs. Today, apart from SFINAE uses where the disambiguation rule doesn't apply, the varargs ellipsis can mainly be found in header files that are intended to be consumed by both C and C++ code, in order to facilitate interoperability between the two languages. In these cases, the declarations must conform to the rules imposed by C syntax, and so they will already conform to the rules proposed here. Lastly, personal experience suggests that the vast majority of C++ users aren't even aware that the comma preceding a varargs ellipsis is optional. [[P0684R2]]</p>
</blockquote>

P3776R1, <em>More trailing commas</em>, proposes trailing commas in function parameter lists but excludes them after C ellipsis parameters. Its proposed grammar preserves the distinction between function parameter packs and C ellipsis parameters. [[P3776R1]]

<aside class=issue>
<p>Agree with Jan on language modes, migration, and trailing-comma wording. Decide whether to remove the old meaning before or alongside homogeneous packs, or use different syntax. Check shared C/C++ headers, macros, and address-taking code. If reclamation is declined, specify a consistent explicit alternative. <a href="https://www.open-std.org/jtc1/sc22/wg21/docs/papers/2025/p3776r1.html">P3776R1</a></p>
</aside>

Reclamation would also require migrating the named comma-less form <code>void send(int value...)</code> to <code>void send(int value, ...)</code>. The name cannot be moved across the ellipsis as an implicit reinterpretation.

## The impact of a breaking change ## {#compatibility-survey}

The survey and Boost analysis below are retained from P1219R2 (2019), not a survey of current code. R2 reports that the ACTCD19 dataset at <a href="https://web.archive.org/web/20190820083052id_/https://codesearch.isocpp.org/">codesearch.isocpp.org (2019 archive)</a> comprised 876,800,403 lines of source code across 2,489,599 files from open-source projects, citing the codesearch FAQ. The survey used it to estimate the impact of requiring a comma before a varargs ellipsis. [[P1219R2]] [[CODESEARCH-FAQ]]

The survey used regular expression searches and human review because a C++ parser with sufficient language support and build scripts for each project were unavailable.

Only headers with extensions <code>.h</code>, <code>.hh</code>, <code>.hpp</code>, or <code>.hxx</code> were considered. Preprocessor directives were stripped from each file before it was run through the C++ preprocessor to remove comments. Removing the macro definitions left invocations unexpanded. Inactive blocks, including <code>#if 0</code>, also remained. Scanning the output for ellipses yielded 258,196 lines, each tagged with its source file.

Lines with identifiers, operators, or other patterns characteristic of pack declarations or expansions were excluded from further analysis.

A comma immediately preceded an ellipsis in 89,614 lines, unambiguously declaring varargs functions.

Of 8,101 lines beginning with an ellipsis, 6,241 appeared to come from function declarations. Of those, 6,092 had a comma immediately before them on a preceding line. Of the remaining 149 lines, 131 were catch clauses or declarations with only an ellipsis. The last 18 mixed static-analysis macros into parameter lists. None of these ellipses is ambiguous with a pack declaration.

<table class="data numeric-table">
<caption>Comma-less varargs survey reported in P1219R2, 2019. [[P1219R2]]</caption>
<thead><tr><th scope=col>Classification</th><th scope=col>Lines</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Lines containing ellipses</td><td>258,196</td></tr>
<tr><td>Varargs ellipses, with comma</td><td>95,724</td></tr>
<tr><td>Varargs ellipses, without comma</td><td>6,318</td></tr>
<tr><td>From Boost</td><td>6,182</td></tr>
<tr><td>From Boost, deduplicated</td><td>879</td></tr>
<tr><td>All others</td><td>136</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

The dataset contained seventeen complete or partial copies of different Boost versions.

Of the 879 unique Boost hits, 416 came from <code>type_traits/detail/is_mem_fun_ptr_tester.hpp</code>. Its <code>is_mem_fun_pointer_tester</code> templates take function pointers with arities from 0 to 24, covering every combination of qualifiers and calling convention, with and without varargs. They detect member function pointer types at compile time. Variadic templates and class template partial specialization can reduce this 2,759-line file to a handful of lines. Most other Boost hits follow a similar pattern to support generic programming in pre-C++11 modes.

<aside class=issue>
<p>Update the code-search survey and Boost analysis using recent data, documenting the dataset and methodology.</p>
</aside>

